    <h1>Pregnancy Week by Week</h1>

    <p>Pregnancy lasts about 40 weeks, counted from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP). This guide tracks what happens to your body, what your appointments look like, and what to watch out for. Your midwife can measure exactly where you are with the <a href="/gestational-age-calculator/">gestational age calculator</a>, and the <a href="/due-date-calculator/">due date calculator</a> projects your estimated delivery date from the same starting point.</p>

    <h2>First Trimester (Weeks 1-13)</h2>

    <p>The first trimester is when most of the body's major structures form, and when pregnancy symptoms tend to be strongest. For you, this stage is mostly about managing nausea, tiredness and a wave of first appointments.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/week-by-week/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 1-4</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> You may not know you are pregnant. Some women experience implantation bleeding.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Fertilization occurs around week 2. The blastocyst implants in the uterine lining by week 4.</p>

    <p>Practically, this is the window to contact maternity services and refer yourself for antenatal care. The NHS recommends referring yourself as soon as you know you are pregnant, so a midwife can start planning your care. Booking early gives you more time to discuss screening tests and your options for giving birth.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 5-8</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Missed period, breast tenderness, nausea, fatigue, frequent urination.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Neural tube forms, heart begins to beat, arm and leg buds appear. Embryo is about the size of a kidney bean by week 8.</p>

    <p>Nausea and vomiting can strike at any time of day or night, not just in the morning. The NHS says morning sickness is common in early pregnancy and usually clears up by weeks 16 to 20, and that it does not put your baby at increased risk.</p>

    <p>Small, frequent meals of plain foods that are high in carbohydrate and low in fat, such as bread, rice, crackers and pasta, can help. Getting plenty of rest helps too, because tiredness can make nausea worse.</p>

    <p>A small number of people develop a severe form called hyperemesis gravidarum. The NHS warns this can lead to dehydration and malnutrition and needs medical treatment. Contact your clinician if you cannot keep fluids down.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/vomiting-and-morning-sickness/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 9-13</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Nausea may peak, fatigue continues, breasts may be tender.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> All major organs have formed. Fingers and toes are visible. Embryo becomes a fetus at week 11 (ACOG).</p>

    <p>Your booking appointment with a midwife usually happens between 8 and 12 weeks and can take around an hour. The midwife checks your blood pressure and urine, offers a blood test for your blood group, and asks about your health, mental health and home situation, as well as exercise, alcohol and smoking. You will be given your maternity notes and information about screening tests and birth options.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>Second Trimester (Weeks 14-27)</h2>

    <p>The NHS describes the second trimester as roughly a third of the way through pregnancy, and many people find their early symptoms ease off here. Your appointments become more regular, and the anatomy scan is the main milestone.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/week-by-week/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 14-16</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Energy returns, nausea fades, you may start to show.</p>

    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 3.5 inches long. Baby can make facial expressions.</p>

    <h3>Weeks 17-20</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Quickening (first movement) typically 16-22 weeks. You may feel fluttering or bubbling.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 6.5 inches long. Baby can hear sounds. Anatomy scan at 18-22 weeks.</p>

    <p>Movements usually start between 16 and 24 weeks. If this is your first baby, you may not feel them until after 20 weeks. Early movements feel like a gentle swirling or fluttering, and you may feel kicks and jerky movements as pregnancy progresses.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 21-24</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Back pain, round ligament pain, heartburn, nasal congestion.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 12 inches long. Baby's lungs are developing. Baby has sleep-wake cycles.</p>

    <p>The NHS lists back pain, headaches, indigestion and heartburn, pelvic pain, piles, stretch marks, and swollen ankles, feet and fingers among the common health problems in pregnancy. If you have not felt your baby move by 24 weeks, tell your midwife. They will check your baby's heartbeat and movements.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 25-27</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Leg cramps, Braxton Hicks contractions, shortness of breath.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 14 inches long. Baby's eyes can open. Baby can respond to light.</p>

    <p>The NHS offers at least 10 antenatal appointments for a first baby, including 2 ultrasound scans, and at least 7 appointments if you have given birth before. Your appointments might be in different places, such as hospitals, GP surgeries or community clinics, and your partner can come to appointments if you would like them to.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <h2>Third Trimester (Weeks 28-40)</h2>

    <p>The NHS counts the third trimester as running from about week 28 to week 41. With around 12 weeks to go, your body works harder, appointments get closer together, and learning your baby's normal movement pattern matters most.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/week-by-week/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 28-32</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Shortness of breath, frequent urination, heartburn, swelling, Braxton Hicks contractions.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 15 inches long. Baby weighs about 2.5 pounds. Baby can open and close eyes.</p>

    <p>Your midwife will check your blood pressure and test your urine at appointments, because high blood pressure in pregnancy can be a sign of the pregnancy condition pre-eclampsia, and urine tests check for infections.</p>

    <p>You should keep feeling your baby move right up to and during labour. There is no set number of movements to expect each day, and the NHS says you do not need to count kicks. What matters is your baby's usual pattern from day to day. If the pattern changes, or movements stop, call your midwife or maternity unit immediately, at any time of day or night.</p>

    <p>The NHS also advises against using a home doppler to check the heartbeat yourself. It is not a reliable way to check your baby's health.</p>

    <p>You can track movement patterns with the <a href="/kick-counter/">kick counter</a>.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/keeping-well/your-babys-movements/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h3>Weeks 33-36</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Lightening (baby dropping), increased pelvic pressure, difficulty sleeping.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is about 17-18.5 inches long. Baby weighs about 4-6 pounds. Baby's lungs are nearly mature.</p>

    <p>Difficulty sleeping is common at this stage, and the NHS lists tiredness and sleep problems among the common symptoms of pregnancy. If you wake frequently, try resting on your side with a pillow between your knees.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/</em></p>

    <h3>Weeks 37-40</h3>
    <p><strong>Your body:</strong> Full term at 37 weeks. You may experience signs of labor: regular contractions, water breaking, bloody show.</p>
    <p><strong>Your baby:</strong> Baby is full term. Baby's organs are fully mature. Baby is about 18.5-20 inches long and weighs about 6-7.5 pounds.</p>

    <p>You can contact maternity services at any time during pregnancy if you are worried about your health or your baby's health. You do not have to wait for your next appointment. If you have referred yourself for pregnancy care and have not been given an appointment, contact local maternity services to ask for one.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/your-pregnancy-care/your-antenatal-care-and-appointments/</em></p>

